# FURTU licence

**This decision has not been made yet, and this file is a placeholder.**

FURTU's own licence was not specified in the brief, and choosing one is a
business decision rather than an engineering one. This file therefore does not
assert a licence. Until it is replaced, the default legal position applies:
**all rights reserved**, nobody is granted permission to copy, modify or
redistribute this work.

## What to do

Pick one and replace this file wholesale.

- **Open source** — MIT or Apache-2.0 both work and both are compatible with
  every dependency in `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md`. MIT is the shortest to adopt and
  is what `pdf-lib`, `js-yaml`, `qrcode` and `fflate` already use, so MIT is the
  frictionless default. Apache-2.0 adds an explicit patent grant.
- **Proprietary** — delete this file and replace it with a copyright notice plus
  whatever terms the client requires. No other file needs to change; nothing in
  the build reads it.

## Note on the dependencies

Whatever is chosen here, it cannot conflict with the shipped dependencies. All
seven runtime libraries are MIT or Apache-2.0, both of which permit relicensing
of the combined work under compatible terms. There is no copyleft obligation
anywhere in `dist/`.

## Dependencies of the design source

The visual design supplied with this project is treated as first-party material
for licensing purposes. If the design actually originated elsewhere, its terms
should be checked before this project is published — see section 3 of
`THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md`.
